What:		/sys/devices/platform/[0-9]+.dpa/state
Date:		May 2025
KernelVersion:	6.12
Contact:	koudai@google.com
Description:
Description:	DPA processor state
		Reports the state of the remote processor, which will be one of:

		- "unloaded"
		- "authenticated"
		- "ncp released"
		- "running"
		- "power off failed"

		"unloaded" means the remote processor is powered off.

		"authenticated" means the firmware image is authenticated and
		waiting to be loaded to DPA.

		"ncp released" means that NCP core is released from the wait
		state and the driver is waiting for the boot completion.

		"running" is the normal state of an available remote processor

		"power off failed" indicates that the driver tried to shutdown
		the firmware but failed. Writing "force stop" to this sysfs
		node will attempt to shutdown DPA forcefully again.

		Writing this file controls the state of the remote processor.
		The following states can be written:

		- "start"
		- "force stop"
		- "unload"

		Writing "start" will attempt to start the DPA with the
		firmwares indicated by, or written to,
		/sys/devices/platform/[0-9]+.dpa/ncp/firmware and
		/sys/devices/platform/[0-9]+.dpa/nep/firmware.
		This is valid when the state is "unloaded" or "authenticated".

		Writing "force stop" will attempt to stop the DPA forcefully.
		This is valid when the state is "ncp released" or "running" or
		"power off failed".

		Writing "unload" will release the resources allocated by DPA
		driver. It is valid when the state is "authenticated". The
		state moves to "unloaded".
		/sys/devices/platform/[0-9]+.dpa/ncp/firmware and
		/sys/devices/platform/[0-9]+.dpa/nep/firmware can be updated
		when the state is in "unloaded".

What:		/sys/devices/platform/[0-9]+.dpa/rpc-(ncp|nep)/check_health
Date:		May 2025
KernelVersion:	6.12
Contact:	mshavit@google.com
Description:
Description:	Check health of remote RPC system
		Probe whether the remote is still responsive to RPCs. Output
		will be one of:

		- "healthy"
		- "unhealthy"

		"healthy" means that the remote responded to our rpc health
		probe.

		"unhealthy" means that we failed to send an RPC to the remote,
		or failed to receive the expected reply.
